      {
         "id": "SECTION_prerequisites",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "<p><span id=\"htZoomImageMsg\"></span></p><p><span id=\"htAmericaAbyaYalaMsg\"></span> (<htcite0>).</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "160fb48c-1711-491e-a1aa-e1257e7889af",
                     "text": "Porto-Gonçalves, Carlos Walter",
                     "page": "pp. 39-43",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2011",
                        "month": "05",
                        "day": "11"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2011",
                     "month": "-1",
                     "day": "-1"
                  }
               ],
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "### Prerequisites\n\nThis text expands your understanding of division (sharing) studied in the previous lessons. Therefore, we recommend reviewing all the previous texts from Chapter 6 in the *First Steps* section of **History Tracers** (<htcite0>) before continuing.\n\nIt also continues the text *Odd or even?* (<htcite1>), where the remainder of the division by 2 showed us which numbers are even and which are odd. Here we extend that idea to the divisions by 1, 3 and 4.",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "b5efc8f9-c268-430f-9681-7a43848ee48e",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "First steps (Volume 1)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "5dee0038-d6c8-404e-8039-5b9a2d11e115",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Odd or even?",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_introduction",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Introduction\n\nWhen we study multiplication tables, we learn that they show the results of repeated addition (<htcite0>). As a result, they do not show the values between the multiplication results.\n\nLet’s recall the 4 times table (<htcite1>):",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "eaa59295-fb80-4c33-b1e4-bcdf3c11dabf",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Adding the same numbers",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "857f1df2-38ce-4da0-9444-686e16ac2314",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Understanding the Multiplication Table (4 and 5)",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2025",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><div id=\"mParent4\" style=\"margin: auto; text-align: center;\"></div></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "In this table, the numbers increase by 4 each time, and as a result, the number 5 does not appear.\n\nWhenever a number appears in a multiplication table, it can be divided by the number of that table without leaving anything extra; whenever it does not appear, the division leaves a remainder, as we saw in the text *Odd or even?* (<htcite0>).\n\nThis suggests an idea: instead of dividing every time, we can learn to recognize those cases by looking at the number itself. This leads us to reflect:",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "5dee0038-d6c8-404e-8039-5b9a2d11e115",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Odd or even?",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 1: Can we see it without dividing?</b><br /><br />We know that 8 belongs to the table of 4 (8/4 = 2) and that 5 does not (5/4 = 1, with 1 left over).<br /><br />Now, how could we tell — without dividing — whether 100, 101, 102 and 103 can be divided by 1, 2, 3 and 4 without leaving anything extra?</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_visual",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Visualizing the Four Rules\n\nLet’s use a table to visualize what we are studying.\n\n#### The Numbers from 1 to 12\n\n**Table 1** shows the numbers from 1 to 12 and, for each of them, whether it belongs to the table of 1, of 2, of 3 or of 4.",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen\"><tr><th>Number</th><th>Table of 1</th><th>Table of 2</th><th>Table of 3</th><th>Table of 4</th></tr><tr><td>1</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td>–</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td>–</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td>–</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td>–</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td>–</td><td>–</td><td>–</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td><td style=\"background-color: lightblue;\">✓</td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 1</b>: The numbers from 1 to 12 checked against the tables of 1, 2, 3 and 4.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "A mark means that the number at the beginning of its row can be divided by the number of that column without leaving anything extra; a dash means that a remainder would appear.\n\nReading **Table 1** column by column, two things stand out:\n- Every row has a mark in the column of 1: there is no number outside the table of 1.\n- Every number marked in the column of 4 is also marked in the column of 2, but not the other way around: 6 and 10 are in the table of 2 and not in the table of 4.\n\nThis is exactly what the rules we are going to build say:",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            }
         ]
      },
      {
         "id": "SECTION_pattern",
         "text": [
            {
               "text": "### Building the Rules\n\nSince each column of **Table 1** repeats the same marks, we do not need the whole table: each column can be summarized in a single rule.\n\n#### Divisible by 1\n\nEvery number is divisible by 1, because the table of 1 contains all the numbers. When we divide any quantity by 1, we keep the same quantity and the remainder is always 0 (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7499239c-c7fc-474e-ad20-1745e19a06d2",
                     "text": "Potgieter, P., & Blignaut, P.",
                     "page": "pp. 1-2",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2018",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen\"><tr><th>5 shared by 1</th><th>Remainder</th></tr><tr><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightyellow; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">0</spam></td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 2</b>: 5 objects shared into 1 group keep all 5 objects; nothing is left over, so the remainder is always 0. The yellow column shows the remainder.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Divisible by 2\n\nA number is divisible by 2 when it is even — that is, when its division by 2 leaves no remainder, as we saw in the text *Odd or even?* (<htcite0>). In practice, it is enough to look at its last digit: 0, 2, 4, 6 or 8 (<htcite1>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "5dee0038-d6c8-404e-8039-5b9a2d11e115",
                     "text": "History Tracers Team",
                     "page": "Odd or even?",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2026",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  },
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7499239c-c7fc-474e-ad20-1745e19a06d2",
                     "text": "Potgieter, P., & Blignaut, P.",
                     "page": "pp. 1-2",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2018",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen\"><tr><th colspan=\"2\">12 shared by 2</th><th>Remainder</th></tr><tr><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightyellow; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">0</spam></td></tr><tr><th colspan=\"2\">13 shared by 2</th><th>Remainder</th></tr><tr><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightyellow; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 3</b>: 12 (last digit 2) splits into two equal groups of 6 with nothing left over, so it is divisible by 2; 13 (last digit 3) leaves 1 left over, so it is not. The yellow column shows the remainder.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Divisible by 3\n\nA number is divisible by 3 when the sum of its digits is divisible by 3. In 12 we add 1 + 2 = 3, and 3 belongs to the table of 3, so 12 is divisible by 3; in 13 we add 1 + 3 = 4, which does not belong to the table of 3, so 13 is not divisible by 3 (<htcite0>).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7499239c-c7fc-474e-ad20-1745e19a06d2",
                     "text": "Potgieter, P., & Blignaut, P.",
                     "page": "pp. 1-2",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2018",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen\"><tr><th colspan=\"3\">12 shared by 3</th><th>Remainder</th></tr><tr><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightyellow; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">0</spam></td></tr><tr><th colspan=\"3\">13 shared by 3</th><th>Remainder</th></tr><tr><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightyellow; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 4</b>: 12 splits into three equal groups of 4 (1 + 2 = 3, which belongs to the table of 3), so it is divisible by 3; 13 leaves 1 left over (1 + 3 = 4, which does not belong to the table of 3), so it is not. The yellow column shows the remainder.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "#### Divisible by 4\n\nA number is divisible by 4 when the number formed by its last two digits is divisible by 4. In 112 the last two digits are 12, and 12 belongs to the table of 4, so 112 is divisible by 4. For a single-digit number we can imagine a zero in front of it: 5 becomes 05, which does not belong to the table of 4 (<htcite0>).\n\nBecause the table of 4 only produces even numbers, every number divisible by 4 is also divisible by 2 (and, like every number, by 1).",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "7499239c-c7fc-474e-ad20-1745e19a06d2",
                     "text": "Potgieter, P., & Blignaut, P.",
                     "page": "pp. 1-2",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2018",
                        "month": "-1",
                        "day": "-1"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><table id=\"sequenceNum\" class=\"tableTen\"><tr><th colspan=\"4\">12 shared by 4</th><th>Remainder</th></tr><tr><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightyellow; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">0</spam></td></tr><tr><th colspan=\"4\">05 shared by 4</th><th>Remainder</th></tr><tr><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightblue; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td><td style=\"height: 100px; background-color: lightyellow; text-align: center;\"><spam class=\"text_to_paint\">|</spam></td></tr></table></p><p class=\"desc\"><b>Table 5</b>: 12 splits into four equal groups of 3, so 112 (last two digits 12) is divisible by 4; 05 splits into four groups of 1 with 1 left over, so 5 is not. The yellow column shows the remainder.</p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p><center><div class=\"first_steps_reflection_parent\"><div class=\"first_steps_reflection question_mark\"><i class=\"fa-solid fa-brain\"></i></div><div class=\"first_steps_reflection\"><b>Reflection 2: Do the rules go together?</b><br /><br />We saw that every number divisible by 4 is also divisible by 2.<br /><br />Is every number divisible by 3 also divisible by 2? Try 6, 9, 12 and 15 to find out.</div></div></center></p>",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "html",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "Not always. 6 and 12 are divisible by both, while 9 and 15 are divisible by 3 only. Each rule looks at a different part of the number — the last digit for 2, the sum of the digits for 3 — so the rules can be combined, but they do not replace one another.\n\n#### Constructions\n\nWhen observing different archaeological sites across the American continent (Abya Yala), we can also find structures that reveal patterns of multiples and remainders.\n\nOne example can be found in the city of Copán, in Honduras (Figure 1).",
               "source": null,
               "date_time": null,
               "isTable": false,
               "imgdesc": "",
               "format": "markdown",
               "PostMention": ""
            },
            {
               "text": "<p class=\"desc\"><img id=\"imgCopanWholeTextSA\" onclick=\"htImageZoom('imgCopanWholeTextSA', '0%')\"  class=\"imgcenter\"/><b>Figure 1</b>: Altar, stela, and the longest hieroglyphic text found at the Copán site (<htcite0>). Photo taken on <htdate1>, during a visit to the site.</p>",
               "source": [
                  {
                     "type": 1,
                     "uuid": "1e007481-9bd3-4a09-ad4d-6e812fe79641",
                     "text": "Instituto Hondureño de Antropologia e Historia",
                     "page": "",
                     "date_time": {
                        "type": "gregory",
                        "year": "2024",
                        "month": "02",
                        "day": "01"
                     }
                  }
               ],
               "date_time": [
                  {
                     "type": "gregory",
                     "year": "2024",
